edition 5;

module spec;

// The flags of `table`, as `toolbox.cli` reads them: name, short, kind, role, default and help, one per `;`. In a file of its own so that the file of the read loops
// stays under 2000 lines, and so that `finish.explain` can read the values of two flags without taking the table as an argument (an argument more in the call, in the read
// loop, cost every one-thread read ~10 instructions a row on arm64: docs/readers.md section 11).

pub fn flag_table() -> [] &static [byte] {
    return "root||path|root||resolve FILE relative to this directory and refuse paths outside it (standard input has no path, so it does not apply to it);delimiter|d|text|none|,|the field separator: a comma, the word tab (or a tab character), or a semicolon;select||text|none||the columns to return, a list: header names separated by commas, or #N for the Nth column (1-based), in the order given, and a repeat repeats the column. A comma, a backslash or a leading # in a name is written with a backslash before it. Examples: --select host,bytes and --select '#3,a\\,b' (the third column, then the column called a,b);where||text|none||only the rows that satisfy this: conditions joined by and (no or, no parentheses), each COLUMN OP VALUE with OP one of = != < <= > >= contains, or COLUMN in (V1, V2). COLUMN is a header name or #N. With no suffix the cell is compared as text, bytewise, so 10 is less than 9. COLUMN:int compares exact integers, COLUMN:dec(N) exact decimals of at most N fractional digits, COLUMN:float doubles. A cell that is not one of those refuses the whole query (value.not-integer and the like), so keep such rows out first: bytes != '' and bytes:int > 5. A name or VALUE with spaces or operators is 'quoted' (a doubled quote is one quote). Examples: --where 'status = 404 and bytes:int > 1000' and --where 'host in (a, b) and path contains x';group||text|none||count the rows by these columns instead of returning them: a list named as for --select, one row per distinct combination, in byte order unless --sort says otherwise. A name may end in :int, :dec(S) or :float to group by the value of the cell and not its text: 1.5 and 1.50 are one group, -0 is 0, the group is written at the column's scale (1.50) or as the shortest decimal that reads back (1.5), and the groups come in numeric order, negatives first. A cell that is not of that type refuses the query (value.not-decimal and the like, context group). A column read as one type must be read as it everywhere in the plan (column.type-conflict). A name that ends in :float is written name\\:float. Examples: --group host and --group host,status and --group 'price:dec(2),status';agg||text|none||what to compute, a comma-separated list of items (default count). An item is count, or FUNCTION:COLUMN with FUNCTION one of sum, min, max, mean, distinct and COLUMN a header name or #N. With no suffix sum, min, max and mean read the cells as exact integers and distinct as text. A suffix on COLUMN says how to read it: :int, :dec(S) for exact decimals of at most S fractional digits (S is 0 to 18, never rounded) or :float for doubles (min, max, distinct, and the exact sum and mean: sum:x:float and mean:x:float give the exact sum, or the exact sum over the count, rounded once to the nearest double, the same for every --threads, and a sum beyond the largest double is agg.float-overflow. A float mean takes no @N, so mean:x:float@2 is agg.bad-spec). mean of integers or decimals is rounded half to even at @N fractional digits written after the column, as mean:bytes@2, and @N may be left out only for a :dec(S) column, whose scale is the default. The output column is the item without its suffixes, as sum:price. A column really called x:dec(2) is written sum:x\\:dec(2). With --group the answer has a row per group, alone one row for all the rows. Examples: --agg count,sum:bytes,max:bytes and --agg 'sum:price:dec(2),mean:bytes@1';sort||text|none||with --group or --agg, order the groups by one output column: count, an aggregate as printed (sum:bytes, mean:price) or a group column, with - before it for descending. Aggregates compare as numbers and group columns bytewise (numerically for a typed --group column), ties go by the group columns. Without it the groups come in byte order of their columns. Examples: --sort -count and --sort sum:bytes;top||nat|none||keep only the first N rows or groups (N at least 1) after sorting: with --order-by the first N sorted rows, with --group or --agg the first N groups in --sort order, so it needs one of them. --limit and --from page inside it. Examples: --top 10 and --top 3;limit||nat|none||the most rows or groups in the answer (default 1000 as json, no limit as csv, ceiling 1000000), for --select, --where, --order-by, --group or --agg. A cut answer has truncated true and data.next.from, the --from of the next page. Examples: --limit 100 and --limit 20 --from 40;from||nat|none||the 0-based row or group to start at, as data.next.from of a truncated answer says, for --select, --where, --order-by, --group or --agg. Each call is a fresh read. Examples: --from 1000 and --from 2000;max-bytes||nat|none|1048576|the most json rows returned hold, past it the answer is truncated with a next (ceiling 67108864);max-groups||nat|none|100000|the most groups kept, one more is limit.too-many-groups (ceiling 1000000);max-distinct||nat|none|100000|the most distinct values kept over all groups for distinct, one more is limit.too-many-distinct (ceiling 10000000);max-state-bytes||nat|none|67108864|the most bytes of group keys, distinct values, sorted rows or the --join file kept (ceiling 1073741824). It counts what the call holds in its tables and rows by a stated formula, which is not resident memory: a join build that is still growing can be about twice that resident (measured 1.65 to 2.26 times on a spike). A join holds, for each row of the --join file, its record bytes plus 16 and, for each distinct key, its bytes plus 60;order-by||text|none||sort the rows by these keys, a comma-separated list: each key is a header name or #N, with - before it for descending and :int, :dec(S) or :float after it to compare by value: exact integers, exact decimals of at most S fractional digits, doubles (otherwise bytewise, so 10 sorts before 9 and an empty cell first). A later key breaks ties of an earlier one, and ties keep the order of the file. A cell that is not of the type refuses the query (value.not-integer, value.not-decimal, value.not-float and the like, context order-by). A name that starts with - or ends in :int, :dec(S) or :float is written \\-name or name\\:int. Rows are held up to --max-sort-rows, so keep it under with --where or --top. Not with --group or --agg. Examples: --order-by -bytes:int and --order-by status,-bytes:int and --order-by '-price:dec(2)';report||text|none||read the whole table once and describe each column's cells instead of returning them: the only value is types. One row per column (--select picks the columns, --where keeps the rows): name, cells, empty, int, dec, float, not_finite, other, dec_max_scale, int_digits_max, widest, suggest, then the row and line of the first empty cell, of the first cell :int would refuse, :dec would refuse and :float would refuse, of the first other cell, that cell's first 64 bytes and whether it was longer. A cell is empty, an int (:int reads it), a dec (a point, no exponent, up to 18 digits: :dec(S) reads it with S the digits after the point), a float (an exponent, or more digits: :float reads it), not_finite (nan, inf) or other. suggest is the smallest declaration every non-empty cell satisfies, written as the suffix: :int, :dec(S) with S the most fractional digits seen, :float, or none (a cell that is not a number, nan or inf, or only empty cells). It is a suggestion and nothing applies it: no flag reads a column as a type unless you say so, and a float column of 0.1-shaped cells is :dec(1), the exact reading. --limit and --from page the columns, --max-bytes bounds a json page, and the counters take 248 bytes a column, counted in --max-state-bytes. Not with --group, --agg, --order-by, --sort or --top. Examples: --report types and --report types --select price,bytes --where 'status = 404';max-sort-rows||nat|none|1000000|the most rows held to sort, one more is limit.too-many-sort-rows (ceiling 20000000);threads||nat|none|1|how many threads read the file (1 to 64), for rows and groups only, on a file of at least --parallel-min-bytes of data: 1 is the sequential read and the answer of every other count is the same bytes. Standard input is always read by one thread (a pipe has no ranges to split), and the answer is still the same bytes;chunk-bytes||nat|none|4194304|the size of the range one thread reads at a time (with --threads), at least 1;parallel-min-bytes||nat|none|1048576|the smallest data (after the header) read by several threads, below it the read is sequential;max-rows||nat|none|10000000|the most data rows read, past it the answer says truncated (ceiling 1000000000);max-line-bytes||nat|none|1048576|the longest line read and the most a kept record may hold, a longer one is limit.line-too-long (ceiling 16777216);format||choice:json/text/csv/jsonl|none|json|json for a program, one document on standard output, text for a person (the shape only, do not parse it), csv for rows or groups as csv (it needs --select, --where, --order-by, --group or --agg), jsonl for the same rows or groups as JSON lines: one object per line, keys the output columns in order (no header line, and two columns of one name are output.duplicate-column), every value a JSON string (a cell that is not UTF-8 is the object {\"b64\": \"...\"}, an empty cell is \"\") except the aggregates (count, sum, min, max, mean, distinct) and a group key with a type (--group NAME:int, NAME:dec(S) or NAME:float), which are JSON numbers written as csv writes them (a key is its value: 007 is 7). csv, jsonl and text are not a json document: standard output is only the csv or the lines, a refusal is one line on standard error as table: RULE: message with the exit status, and rows already written stand, so a non-zero exit means the output is a prefix;query||text|none||ask in a small subset of SQL, translated to the flags it stands for and run as they would be. select COLUMNS, or *, or count(*), sum(c), min(c), max(c), mean(c) or mean(c, N), count(distinct c), then optionally from 'FILE' or from stdin, where COND and COND, group by COLUMNS, order by KEY desc, limit N offset N. A COND is c = 'x', c != 5, c <> 5, c < 5 (also <=, > and >=), c contains 'x' or c in ('a', 'b'): only and, no or and no parentheses. A value is 'single quoted' text (a doubled quote is one quote) or a bare number. Text compares bytewise, so write cast(c as int) > 5 or c::int > 5 to compare numbers. The types are int, dec(N) and float. A name is bare (letters, digits and underscore, and not a keyword) or \"double quoted\" (spaces, commas, dots, keywords), and #3 is the third column. Keywords are not case sensitive and names are. A grouping selects its group by columns first, in the same order, then aggregates, and has at least one. A query is at most 4096 bytes. There are no aliases, expressions, joins, like, null or select distinct: each is refused as query.unsupported naming the feature and what to write instead, and query.syntax gives the byte offset and what was expected. Do not mix it with --select, --where, --group, --agg, --sort, --order-by, --rows or --report. --limit and --from stay flags unless the query has its own limit or offset, and --top stays a flag. A from in the query names the file, so then there is no FILE operand. Examples: --query \"select host, count(*) group by host order by count(*) desc\" and --query \"select host, bytes where status = 404 order by bytes::int desc limit 5\";rows||bool|none||every row with all its columns, no condition: the form of select * with nothing else. It is the way to ask for a whole table as rows when there is no --select, --where or --group to say so, and it takes none of those, nor --order-by or --report. Example: --rows --format csv;explain||bool|none||print the plan the other flags (or --query) resolve to, as one JSON line, and do not run it: the source and how it is read, the columns with their positions, every condition, grouping, aggregate and order with its type, the page, the work the plan is (rows read, what is held, the bounds in force), the threads it would use and why, the rules the data could still refuse it with, and notes. A file is opened and its first 64 KiB read for the header, and nothing after the header is read. Standard input is not read at all, so its columns are not known. A plan the run would refuse before it read a record (a bad flag or query, a name the header lacks, a file that is missing, a header past --max-line-bytes) is refused with the error and the exit status of the run. Example: --explain --group status --agg sum:bytes sales.csv;input-format||choice:csv/jsonl/ndjson|none|csv|how the input is read: csv (the default, a header row names the columns), or jsonl, also spelled ndjson: JSON lines, one JSON object per line, whose columns are declared with --fields (never guessed from the name or the content of the input). A line is parsed as strict JSON (RFC 8259) and a line that is not an object is parse.jsonl-not-object. A number is the cell its exact text is, never through a double (the typed reads :int, :dec(S) and :float then read it as they read a csv cell), a string its decoded text, true and false the words. Standard input and a file give the same answer, and a file is read by --threads as a csv file is. Not with --delimiter, which then only sets the delimiter of csv output. Example: --input-format jsonl --fields id,status,user.name --where 'status:int >= 500' events.jsonl;fields||text|none||with --input-format jsonl, the columns, by path, a comma-separated list: a path is keys separated by dots, and a number in the path is an index where the value is an array: id, user.name, items.0.sku. A backslash escapes the next byte, which must be a comma, a backslash, a # or a dot (a key with a dot is a\\.b). The column is called by the path as written, and --select, --where, --group, --agg, --order-by and #N use that name. Every declared path is looked up in every line, used or not, so declare what the plan reads. A value that is a string, a number, true or false is the cell. null, or a key or index that is not there, is the absent cell (see --absent). an object or an array is parse.jsonl-not-scalar. a key twice on the way to a declared path is parse.jsonl-duplicate-key. Required with --input-format jsonl (input.fields-required), and not with csv. Example: --fields id,status,user.name,tags.0;absent||choice:refuse/empty|none|refuse|with --input-format jsonl, what a declared path with no value is (null, a key or an index that is not there, a step through something that is not an object or an array): refuse (the default) is parse.jsonl-missing-field, naming the line, the row, the column and the reason, and empty is the empty cell (which the typed reads then refuse as an empty csv cell is refused: bytes != '' and bytes:int > 5). Not with csv;max-json-nodes||nat|none|4096|with --input-format jsonl, the most JSON nodes a line may hold (a string, a number, a literal, a key, an array or an object is one), 1 to 1048576: the tape of a reader is three integers a node, made once. a line with more is limit.json-too-many-nodes. Not with csv;discover||bool|none||with --input-format jsonl, instead of a plan, a bounded report of what the first lines hold, as data.discover: each path to depth 8 in order of first appearance, written as --fields takes it, with how many of the examined lines have a string, an int (a number with no point or exponent), a number, a bool, a null, an empty object, an array or nothing there, and the line it first appears on. lines_examined, complete (the input ended inside --discover-lines), paths_truncated (more than --max-fields paths), undeclarable (a path that --fields cannot say: an empty key) and arrays (the paths that hold an array, which is not expanded: declare tags.0 for the first). Nothing in it reaches a plan: copy the paths you want into --fields. Takes no plan flag, no --fields and no --format csv. Example: --input-format jsonl --discover events.jsonl;discover-lines||nat|none|1000|with --discover, how many records to examine (1 to 1000000);max-fields||nat|none|256|with --discover, how many paths to list (1 to 4096), past it paths_truncated is true;join||path|path-read||the build side of a join: a second CSV file beneath --root, resolved as FILE is (- is refused, since it is read whole before the first probe row). FILE (or standard input) is the probe side and is streamed, this file is held in memory and counted in --max-state-bytes. The answer is the inner join, rows only: for each probe row, in the probe order, one row for each row of this file whose key is equal to the probe row key, in the order of this file, the cells of the probe row and then the cells of the build row (a probe row with no match is not in it). It needs --on. --select, --where, --limit, --from and --format work on the joined row, and --group, --agg, --order-by, --sort, --top and --report do not yet, so pipe the join as csv into a second call. The first byte of output waits for the whole build, --threads reads on one core with the same bytes, and nothing is written to disk. Example: --join users.csv --on user=id events.csv;on||text|none||with --join, the key as PROBE=BUILD: a column of FILE and a column of the --join file, each a header name or #N, whose cells are equal when they are the same bytes (text: 007 is not 7, a quoted cell is the text inside its quotes, and an empty cell matches an empty cell, since there is no null). One pair and no type suffix in this version. A comma, a backslash, an equals sign, a colon or a leading # in a name is written with a backslash before it. A name that both files have is join.column-collision unless --join-prefix is given. Examples: --on user=id and --on #2=name;join-type||choice:inner|none|inner|with --join, which join: inner, the only one in this version, is each probe row once for every build row with its key;join-prefix||any|none||with --join, text put before every column name of the --join file in the answer, so that files with a column of the same name can be joined (--join-prefix d_ makes the id column of the build file d_id, and it is named d_id in --select and --where). Without it a name that both files have is join.column-collision. Example: --join-prefix d_;max-join-rows||nat|none|10000000|with --join, the most rows the join may produce before --where, 1 to 1000000000: one more is limit.too-many-join-rows, which says how many build rows the probe row key has (a key that repeats in the --join file multiplies the probe rows). A --limit that is met stops the probe early";
}
